# Components of an Analytics Rule

## Rule Name

The name should clearly describe the detection.

Example:

```
Multiple SSH Authentication Failures
```

---

## Description

Provide a concise explanation of:

- Detection objective
- Data source
- Expected behavior
- Investigation guidance

Good documentation helps analysts quickly understand why an alert was generated.

---

## KQL Query

The KQL query contains the detection logic.

Example:

```kusto
MeridianLogs_CL
| where RawData has "Failed password"
| extend SourceIP = extract(@"from ([0-9.]+)", 1, RawData)
| summarize Attempts = count() by SourceIP
| where Attempts > 5
```

---

## Query Frequency

Defines how often the rule executes.

Examples:

- Every 5 minutes
- Every 15 minutes
- Hourly

Higher frequencies improve detection speed but may increase resource usage.

---

## Lookup Period

Specifies the time window evaluated during each execution.

Examples:

- Last 5 minutes
- Last hour
- Last 24 hours

The lookup period should align with the expected attack pattern.

---

## Alert Threshold

Determines when an alert is generated.

Examples:

- More than 5 failed logins
- More than 10 firewall blocks
- More than 20 malware detections

Thresholds should balance sensitivity and false-positive reduction.

---

## Severity

Each alert should have an appropriate severity.

| Severity | Typical Use Case |
|----------|------------------|
| Informational | Routine monitoring |
| Low | Minor anomalies |
| Medium | Suspicious activity |
| High | Likely malicious behavior |
| Critical | Immediate response required |

---

## Entity Mapping

Entity mapping links alerts to security-relevant objects.

Common entities include:

- User accounts
- Hosts
- IP addresses
- Processes
- URLs
- File hashes

Proper entity mapping improves investigation efficiency and visualization.

---

## MITRE ATT&CK Mapping

Rules should be mapped to relevant ATT&CK techniques whenever possible.

For this project:

| Technique | ID |
|-----------|----|
| Brute Force | T1110 |
| Valid Accounts | T1078 |

MITRE mapping standardizes detection coverage and reporting.

